      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'd be happy to help you categorize those social media trends. Here's a rough ranking from most sophisticated to most street/niche: \n\n- Rich words with classical influences (e.g. \"elevated,\" \"luxe,\" or \"gilded\") tend to feel fancy and high-end.\n- Fashion and luxury-focused slang (e.g. \"sponcon,\" \"influencer marketing,\" \"fashion inspiration\") might feel somewhat aspirational but still accessible to a general audience.\n- Terms with strong online or gaming roots (e.g. \"vibin',\" \"lowkey,\" or \"highkey\") have a more youth-oriented feel and may not resonate with older audiences.\n- Newly emerging slang related to social justice or activist movements (e.g. \" burn it down,\" \" identity politics\") tends to be highly niche and may polarize audiences.\n-imitives and ignorant direct references (e.g. \"funny fat guy\") generally have a more excluded audience attractiveness",
